<section class="faq">
  <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
  <details>
    <summary>What is a robots.txt file?</summary>
    <p>A robots.txt file sits at the root of your website (example.com/robots.txt) and tells search engine crawlers which pages they can and cannot access. It follows the Robots Exclusion Standard (REP-1).</p>
  </details>
  <details>
    <summary>Should I block all crawlers or just specific ones?</summary>
    <p>Most sites should allow all crawlers (<code>User-agent: *</code>) and only block specific paths like <code>/admin/</code> or <code>/tmp/</code>. Blocking all crawlers with <code>Disallow: /</code> will prevent your site from appearing in search results.</p>
  </details>
  <details>
    <summary>Does robots.txt prevent content from being indexed?</summary>
    <p>No! robots.txt only prevents crawling, not indexing. If other sites link to a disallowed page, Google may still index it based on the linking page's context. To prevent indexing, use <code>&lt;meta name="robots" content="noindex"&gt;</code> or HTTP headers instead.</p>
  </details>
  <details>
    <summary>Where do I place the robots.txt file?</summary>
    <p>At the root of your domain: <code>https://example.com/robots.txt</code>. It must be accessible at this exact path for crawlers to find it.</p>
  </details>
</section>
